\title{Sets as fields}
\import{prelude}

\p{An element \code{A : \kwi{Set}} may be used directly as a type. Elements of \code{\kwi{Set}} are supposed to correspond to collections of things, so an element of \code{A} as a type is just an element of the collection \code{A} is supposed to be. For example, consider the following theory.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} TourWithGuests := \kw{sig}
  Child : \kwi{Set}
  Adult : \kwi{Set}
  charlie : Child
  grandpa-joe : Adult
\kw{end}
}

\p{An element \code{m} of \code{TourWithGuests} consists of two collections, \code{m.Child} and \code{m.Adult}, along with specific elements \code{m.grandpa-joe} in \code{m.Adult} and \code{m.charlie} in \code{m.Child}.}

\p{So, there are three layers of things here that we must be careful to distinguish:
\ol{
  \li{The universe \code{\kwi{Set}} which is the abstract type of collections,}
  \li{A particular set \code{m.Child : \kwi{Set}} which is specified by the model \code{m},}
  \li{A particular element of that set \code{m.charlie : m.Child}, also specified by the model \code{m}.}
}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Child}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Adult}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{a1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{a2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.grandpa-joe}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Adult}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{a1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.charlie}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Child}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{The \code{root.grandpa-joe} and \code{root.charlie} tables each have exactly one row.}
    \li{The value in \code{root.grandpa-joe.a} is a row ID in \code{root.Adult}.}
    \li{The value in \code{root.charlie.a} is a row ID in \code{root.Child}.}
  }
  \p{Here, \code{grandpa-joe} selects \code{a1} from \code{root.Adult}, and \code{charlie} selects \code{c1} from \code{root.Child}.}
}

\p{In particular, records in Coln may be \em{dependent}: the types of later fields may refer to earlier fields, as is the case for \code{grandpa-joe} and \code{charlie}.}
